speechtext:
I think I can form as good a judgment as to what is a fair and honest transaction as he can, and I decline to allow that hon. gentleman to characterize this appropriation in the manner in which he has done. I have to apply my judgment upon this question, and I say that the hon. gentleman has no right to say that this is not a just and honest claim. I say that he has no business to express an opinion such as he has expressed when he does not know what the facts are. I venture the opinion that this $10,000 item that we are asking parliament to revote is as honest and as square a claim as was ever paid by any government of this country- When the hon. gentleman talks about my decency, what does he mean ? Is it a part of the duty of a minister of the Crown, who is at the head of a department, to apply his judgment to a question of this kind, and to give every claim that is presented to him the benefit of the best judgment he can form, or is it not ? Because some hon. gentleman who has some political or party end to serve denounces it in strong terms without knowing what he is talking about, must the minister withdraw such a claim ? No, Sir, I should be ashamed of myself if, when I had a claim presented to me of $10,000, which is a proper and just claim, I hesitated about paying it. The hon. gentleman says that the Auditor General thinks that this claim should not be paid. The Auditor General expresses that -opinion in regard to almost every item that comes before him. The Auditor General feels that it is part of his duty to obstruct the payment of claims upon every possible, conceivable, technical or frivolous ground. If the Auditor General had his way he would make the government of this country absolutely impossible.
That is what I say with regard to that. I have no doubt that the Auditor General thinks he is discharging his duty, but. in my opinion, he has a most exaggerated idea of his duty when he undertakes to set up his opinion against the opinion of engineers and competent men-as competent as he is- 177}
and consider that his judgment is superior to them all, and superior to parliament on the top of all. Parliament voted this money. If I mistake not, we had three days' debate on this and the collateral claims of the Gilbert Dredging Company. Parliament, with a knowledge of all the facts, voted that money. I want to know what authority the Auditor General had to say that he should judge whether parliament should have voted that money or not. It is true that he had Mr. Lash to agree with him. The opinion of Mr. Lash is, no doubt, usually very good. Mr. Lash's opinion upon practical questions, as well as upon legal questions, is, I dare say, as good as anybody else's, but it is no better. Mr Lash'-s opinion was submitted to the Department of Justice. The Department of Justice went into it very carefully, and the Department of Justice came to the conclusion that Mr. Lash's opinion was of no weight ; ought not to govern, and that Mr. Lash was wrong upon the questions which he undertook to pass upon. I do not pretend to have any professional knowledge of the rank, character or standing that Mr. Lash has, but, such as I have, and applying it to the question, I had no hesitation in saying, not a particle of hesitation in saying, that Mr. Lash was wrong. I believe that nine out of ten men in this parliament, who view with a open mind all the particulars of this claim, will say that Mr. Lash was wrong, and that the Auditor General was wrong. 1 have no doubt about it myself. My hon. friend (Mr. Belcourt), who represented these gentlemen, presented the case to the Auditor General, but the Auditor General refused to pay. He put obstacles in the way. He positively declined to pay four or five of the items included in this estimate, did he not ?
